{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 20,
  "title": "In the garden",
  "description": "Name the things in a garden, and say what you do there.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "A garden is a place with living things in it, so it has its own nouns. And almost everything you do in a garden is an -ar verb: regar, plantar, cortar, mirar.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The nouns",
        "body": "El árbol, el arbusto, el riego and el huerto are masculine. La flor, la hierba, la tierra, la semilla, el guante and la tijera are not all the same: el guante is masculine and la tijera is feminine, even though both end in -e. The ending is a guide, not a rule.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "el árbol",
            "en": "the tree"
          },
          {
            "es": "la flor",
            "en": "the flower"
          },
          {
            "es": "la tijera",
            "en": "the scissors"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "What you do there",
        "body": "An -ar verb drops the -ar and takes the ending that matches the person: yo riego, tú riegas, él riega. The plural of these nouns works as you saw: el árbol becomes los árboles and la flor becomes las flores.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "riego las flores",
            "en": "I water the flowers"
          },
          {
            "es": "los árboles son grandes",
            "en": "the trees are big"
          },
          {
            "es": "hay una semilla en la tierra",
            "en": "there is a seed in the earth"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
